  {
    "episode_id": 90034,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90034.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 83,
    "turn_text": "I think that's right. Major, one of the things that as this debate has played out is there's been this loss to be able to hold these two ideas in your head at the same time, right? One, it says, look, certification was the right solution that day, that time, good on it. I was proud of the way that ended that day. It was an ugly day on the Capitol. But proud of how it ended that day. We fulfilled the constitutional responsibility of that day. But I look at what how Pennsylvania changed their laws inside the window. I look at these enormous changes in mail in ballots. And I think of the election offices back home in Kansas. how they're resourced. But when you make those big changes in tight to an election, you just amplify risk. And when you amplify risk, transparency goes away. And so people doubt and theories arise and people aren't confident in our system. That's tragic and must be prevented. And a couple of states have made things a little bit better if I have that right. But a lot of states haven't done what's going to need to be done to make sure that we don't have this happen again right we had an election stolen in 1960. Right, this is that people act like this is sweet generous like we've never had a contested election in the United States before. And I say that sadly we shouldn't have. elections where people have dealt with. It's 2022. You can figure out who's allowed to vote. You can make sure that they get a chance to vote if they want to. One time you count that vote one time and you get the data published very quickly, very accurately so that everybody can have confidence that the person who is, you know, Sally who at City Council or Johnny who's the County Commissioner or someone running for the highest office, we all know that they were the rightfully elected person.",
