    "turn_text": "be? We had violence in the summer of 20 that was deeply inconsistent with who we are as American people in law enforcement was told they should be defunded and the courts refused to prosecute too many of those cases. So I'm always concerned that there will be. I'm very concerned when you politicize your first comment that you heard about a fascist court It's deeply troubling to hear people talk about that institution, that really special place that is supposed to be above the political fray. I'm not a novice either. I get the politics around the Supreme Court, too. But it is really important that we not rip at the veneer surrounding those nine people who are designated and tasked with just interpreting a document. And so, sure, I suppose there's risk there'll be violence. I hope there's not. I hope if there is that everyone who commits unlawful violence is prosecuted to the full extent of the law, that would be appropriate, whichever side of that particular debate the violent person is on. And then, you know, as for the ruling itself, I've long thought that Roe created exactly this kind of strife. I've believed deeply that Roe took away from the political process, the capacity to have reason to bait on what is a deeply held view for so many Americans. I hold this view intensely. I believe in the right for every child to be born with all of my heart. I believe it because of science and I believe it because of my faith. And Roe took away the capacity for us to make that case and to argue this out in the process that I think our founders would have preferred. And so if Roe, if Roe in fact is overturned in a week or so, I think that will return this to the states. They'll be heated debate. I hope it's within the lanes of argument and reason. And then I hope, I hope every state will choose to protect the unborn every way they can.",
