    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90034.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 110,
    "turn_text": "I don't know how to predict how the meetings will go. It's unfortunate that President Biden put himself in a place today where he's had to walk back a central thesis of his campaign. That's always dangerous. One of the central thesis was, I'm a different kind of guy. I care about human rights. I'm going to make the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia a pariah state. And then it walked up and smacked him in the face, right? Reality. The hard truth of protecting America and making America prosperous was something Joe Biden told the American people a lie about. And that's too bad, because now he's having to go live the truth, which is that we are connected to these nations in deep and fundamental ways, the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia. is an important security partner for the United States of America. We're on the cusp of beginning them to sign a peace agreement with Israel. They are headed in the right direction. They have a human rights record that none of us would want in our own country. I get that. The murder of Jamal Khashoggi was a terrible action. But the reality is, and President Biden is facing that, as he's headed to the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia, where he's going to have to explain to a lot of people, not just to confront Salman Mohammed bin Salman, but to a lot of people, why it is his view changed from what he promised the American people and what he's doing now. I actually think he has it right. He shouldn't have to be in exactly this position. But if you have green climate theology in your blood, you end up more dependent on others for energy. And we certainly have that in this administration. The good people of Kansas and Oklahoma and North Dakota and the Bakken Shale and Midland Texas are suffering for that.",
